WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.680
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:02.840
So, you might remember that when I was a little kid,

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.900
I was diagnosed with a learning disability, a sensory dysfunction.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.980
Basically, sensory disorders are when you either have a hard time dealing with the information you have coming into you,

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.600
or you have too much information, you're not getting enough information.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.640
And when I say information here I'm talking about stuff coming into your senses.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.060
Not like stuff you're learning, but just everything coming at you all the time.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:28.420
If I had been born ten years later, I probably would have been additionally or alternately diagnosed with ADHD.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:33.760
I can't say for sure, of course, but when I was seven years old, which is the average age for an ADHD diagnosis,

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.020
attention-deficit disorders just weren't being diagnosed or discussed as much.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.180
I definitely had symptoms that don't fit in the attention-deficit box

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.820
but are sometimes overlapping conditions like mild dyslexia and motor control problems.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.100
On the other hand, I definitely do have some attention issues.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:53.160
Schedules can be really frustrating for me, I have a hard time being in a place where I'm supposed to be at the right time.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.080
Everyone who works with me can tell you that. I'm sorry, everyone.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.180
When I am able to focus on things, which is actually pretty frequently, I get /really/ focused,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.960
and when I am forced to disengage from that focus, I get irrationally angry.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.760
I'm always searching for new things to create, you might have also noticed that,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.425
and I wear holes really fast in my socks

00:01:09.425 --> 00:01:10.840
because my toes never stop moving.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:11.700
They're going right now.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:19.600
I've gotten pretty good at managing these things, with the help of friends and family and tutors and teachers and, thank god, an assistant now.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.200
How did I ever function without help?

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:30.760
But I still often hurt people's feelings or annoy them by going somewhere else in my head when people that I care about are talking to me.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.680
As I have previously said, I'm often late for stuff, which makes people think that I don't care about them.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.180
And sometimes I have very difficult to control flashes of rage in grocery lines.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.300
But here's a general thing about psychological disorders (which we're discussing now on our new channel 'Sci Show Psych' by the way!!) :

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.360
disorders are only classified as disorders when they're ongoing, frustrating impairments.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:54.200
And I, especially now, am less impaired by the different way that my brain maybe works.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:02.360
That's because of great support from family and from teachers, and it's because I've gotten myself into situations where the different way that my brain works is actually maybe an advantage

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.160
rather than a hindrance, and it's also because of, like, economics.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:11.820
My parents took me to a handwriting therapist who was like "Can you get this kid a computer? Because he needs to learn how to type"

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:17.440
like this person is a professional person who makes people write better and they're like "Get this child a computer!"

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.300
And the fact that getting a computer was an option for my family back in like the 80s,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.200
and that then I had this tool in my home that none of my other classmates had

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.780
that allowed me to develop a skillset that none of my other classmates had,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:35.620
all those things are huge. There's kind of two parts to minimising the negative effects of your brain not working normal:

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.340
changing the environment to suit the brain, and then there's improving the functionality of the brain itself

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.900
through things like medication or mindfulness or exercise.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.340
Because of the advantages I had I've been pretty successful at doing that first thing,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.480
which means I kinda maybe didn't ever have a hard enough time to look into the second thing.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.700
So, do I have ADHD? Maybe.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.600
But I am definitely dedicated to doing my best to understand my own shortcomings

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.880
and to develop techniques and habits to deal with them.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.700
And I've learned some of those habits by living inside of my own body,

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.680
but I've learned others from people who have ADHD

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.800
and are talking openly and honestly about it, and also from people who work with people with attention disorders.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:19.400
I think by now I've gotten through the hardest parts of living in my brain, you know?

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:24.500
Like figuring out how to cope for all the ways in which my mind isn't perfect for the situations it finds itself in.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.620
I am proud of that. I think that it took a lot of hard work, but it also took a lot of help.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.000
Help that I got in spades because of my economic advantages,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.980
because I was diagnosed with a sensory processing disorder early on,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.420
and I had a lot of great tutors.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.660
In short, I've been very lucky and I've had lots of help.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.560
If you think that you might have ADHD I have a video to recommend for you.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.160
It's very good, it was the thing that inspired me to make this.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.260
Figuring out how to live in your own mind and your own body is, like, the task of your life.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.080
And there's no reason to think that you have to take that on alone.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.920
John, I'll see you on Tuesday!

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.113
This, by the way, is the reason why there's blue paint all over my hoodie.

